**Insert after line 85** (the 'JSON-only JWT claims authorize current owner' assertion) and before line 86 `reset role;`:
```sql
select pg_temp.set_review_jwt(pg_temp.n(3));
select ok(engineering_private.engineering_access(pg_temp.n(4),pg_temp.n(7)),'project peer holds enabled engineering access');
select throws_ok($$select public.api_read_native_step_review(pg_temp.n(31),pg_temp.task(1,31),pg_temp.step_review_snapshot())$$,
  pg_temp.review_error(),'ovd563_review_unavailable','same-project peer cannot retrieve owner STEP');
reset role;
update engineering_private.engineering_operators set enabled=false where organization_id=pg_temp.n(4) and user_id=pg_temp.n(1);
set local role authenticated;
select pg_temp.set_review_jwt(pg_temp.n(1));
select throws_ok($$<same read>$$,pg_temp.review_error(),'ovd563_review_unavailable','revoked owner access returns no STEP');
reset role;
update engineering_private.engineering_operators set enabled=true where organization_id=pg_temp.n(4) and user_id=pg_temp.n(1);
set local role authenticated;
select pg_temp.set_review_jwt(pg_temp.n(1));
select is(pg_temp.review_status(public.api_read_native_step_review(pg_temp.n(31),pg_temp.task(1,31),pg_temp.step_review_snapshot())),'ready','restored owner access retrieves STEP again');
```

**Why this is valid**
- Lines 82-83 leave `request.jwt.claim.sub` empty and the claims JSON set to n(1). `set_review_jwt` sets `claim.sub`, which takes precedence in auth.uid(), so the explicit call for n(3) is required.
- `authenticated` holds USAGE on engineering_private (20260910045917:7) and EXECUTE on engineering_access (:117), so the positive control is callable.
- The step bytes are still intact at this point; they are corrupted from line 87.

